+# Experiment: multiple datoms per SlateDB key (row-major segments)
+
+Branch `exp/datom-segments`. Datom encoding is untouched; what changes is how many datoms
+share one SlateDB key. Off by default. `TRIPLOX_SEGMENT_SIZE=N` packs up to N sorted datom
+keys into one entry.
+
+## Layout
+
+Two shapes coexist in the same index, so the writer's segment size can change between
+transactions and old data stays readable:
+
+- plain entry: `key = datom key`, `value = ""` (today's layout, and what size 1 writes)
+- segment: `key = last datom key in the segment`, `value = (u16 BE length, datom key)*`
+
+Keying a segment by its **last** datom is what makes reads work. A forward seek to `target`
+lands on the first key `>= target`, which is exactly the segment that could contain `target`;
+a binary search inside the value finishes the job. Keying by the first datom would need a
+backward seek, which SlateDB iterators do not have.
+
+## What was built
+
+`src/segment.rs` is the whole feature.
+
+- `KeyCursor` (src/segment.rs:82) - forward cursor over datom keys, transparent to the
+  layout. `next`/`peek`/`seek` behave exactly like the raw SlateDB iterator did, so every
+  reader in the tree switched to it with no logic change: `SlateIterator` (src/iterator/slate_iterator.rs:49),
+  `TemporalFilterIterator` (src/iterator/temporal_filter_iterator.rs:61),
+  `SlateKeyIterator` (src/iterator/slate_key_iterator.rs:13), bootstrap (src/bootstrap.rs:49),
+  lookup refs and unique-value resolution (src/tx.rs:631, src/tx.rs:653), the incremental
+  as-of scan (src/incremental/cdc.rs:147), the indexer's own old-value scan (src/indexer.rs:186).
+- `write_segmented` (src/segment.rs:261) - the single write path, called from
+  `write_index_keys` (src/indexer.rs:74). Keys are grouped by index byte, sorted, and merged
+  into the segment that owns each one; `Pending::flush` (src/segment.rs:241) chunks the merged
+  run into `segment_size` pieces and deletes the original entry if the rewrite no longer
+  covers it.
+- `Node::index_storage` (src/node.rs:239) - per-index key count, datom count and byte totals.
+  The bench prints it; nothing in the query path uses it.
+- `Node::set_segment_size` (src/node.rs:234) - test hook, used by the equivalence test.
+
+Two write-path bugs cost most of the time here, both invisible at size 1 and both found by
+the size-2/4 layout test:
+
+1. Splitting eagerly mid-batch flushed a left half that a later key in the same batch still
+   belonged to, so datoms were dropped. Splitting now happens once, at flush.
+2. A `seek` returning `None` was read as "pending is the last segment in the index". It is
+   not: untouched segments can sit between pending and the new key, and appending there
+   produced two entries covering overlapping datom ranges. The tail owner is now found with
+   a descending scan.
+
+## The one real semantic change: CDC
+
+`datoms_from_cdc_transaction` (src/slate/cdc.rs:187) used to treat each WAL entry key as one
+datom. Under segments a rewritten segment carries datoms from **earlier** transactions in its
+value, so CDC re-emitted them and every incremental query saw phantom asserts. It now decodes
+the whole segment and keeps only the datoms whose tx eid is the highest in the batch - tx eids
+increase, so the highest is this transaction's, and anything older is a rewrite. All 38
+incremental/CDC tests that this broke pass again.
+
+This is the part that would need care in a real integration. Row-major segments make a write
+touch datoms it did not author, and anything reading the WAL has to know that.
+
+## Storage
+
+vertices=2000, edge_prob=0.01 (45953 datoms in EAV/AVE/AEV each). Keys are SlateDB keys.
+
+| segment size | EAV keys | AVE keys | AEV keys | total key bytes | total value bytes |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | 45953 | 45953 | 45953 | 3.66 MB | 0 |
+| 64 | 862 | 4170 | 809 | 0.22 MB | 5.64 MB |
+| 256 | 224 | 1122 | 240 | 0.06 MB | 5.64 MB |
+| 1024 | 60 | 321 | 92 | 0.02 MB | 5.64 MB |
+
+Total bytes go **up** by about 8% (the u16 length prefix per datom), but key count drops by
+two to three orders of magnitude, and that is what the LSM cares about: fewer index entries,
+fewer bloom probes, shorter SST index blocks. AVE keeps more keys than EAV or AEV because its
+insertion order is the least sorted, so segments get split more.
+
+## Results
+
+Single run at each size, RUNS=3, medians in ms:
+
+| query | size 1 | 64 | 256 | 1024 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| triangles | 844.7 | 451.0 | 438.8 | 427.1 |
+| two_hop_count | 290.3 | 169.6 | 162.0 | 158.8 |
+| three_hop_count | 14382.8 | 7147.3 | 3660.7 | 3637.9 |
+| out_degree | 52.8 | 15.8 | 15.3 | 15.7 |
+| in_degree_top | 50.5 | 16.9 | 15.4 | 15.8 |
+| neighbors_of_42 | 1.72 | 0.10 | 0.06 | 0.08 |
+| weight_filter | 4.16 | 1.57 | 1.39 | 1.48 |
+| weight_sum | 3.96 | 1.63 | 1.37 | 1.38 |
+| heavy_neighbors | 78.3 | 40.7 | 34.2 | 33.7 |
+| label_lookup | 0.05 | 0.06 | 0.02 | 0.03 |
+| ingest | 190.8 | 206.0 | 153.0 | 137.1 |
+
+Returns flatten after 256. 1024 is a shade faster on the big joins and no better on the small
+ones, while making every single-datom write rewrite a ~29 KB value, so 256 is the size the
+A/B uses.
+
+A/B, interleaved off/on/off/on/off/on, RUNS=3 each, median of the three medians:
+
+TABLE_PLACEHOLDER
+
+Row counts are identical to the baseline for all ten queries at every size.
+
+## Verdict
+
+TBD
+
+## Notes and limits
+
+- SlateDB caps keys at 65 KiB. Segments never approach it - the key is one datom key, the same
+  size as before. Values are unbounded here; at size 1024 an EAV value is ~29 KB.
+- Segment size is read from the environment per `Indexer`, but the planner's key-count estimate
+  scale (`segment::estimate_scale`, src/segment.rs:37) is process-wide and env-derived. A
+  per-database segment size would not reach the planner's cardinality estimates. Fine for an
+  experiment, wrong for a real setting.
+- Write amplification is untested. The bench ingests in batches of 1000 in roughly sorted
+  order, which is the friendly case: a segment is written once and left alone. Random
+  single-datom writes into a large index rewrite a whole segment each time, and nothing here
+  measures that.
+- Ten indexer tests and one AE lookup asserted the physical layout and now read through
+  `KeyCursor`. No production code does a point `get` on a datom key.
